  "body": "While the input account frames this as a coordination mechanics failure, an alternative view locates the friction in undefined quality standards rather than task allocation. Arguments about chores often persist even when roles are assigned because flatmates hold different implicit definitions of what constitutes completion. The reported avoidance may signal defensiveness about performance rather than fatigue with scheduling discussions. Addressing this requires shifting focus from who does the work to what counts as done. A visual standard or checklist might reduce the need for verbal negotiation entirely, bypassing the stressed communication channel identified in the input account.",
  "commitments": "Taking this view up involves assuming that semantic ambiguity lies in task definitions rather than assignment agreements. This contrasts with the input account's emphasis on separating negotiation from execution; instead, it suggests reducing the need for negotiation by standardizing output. Implementing this requires attempting to define visible standards without triggering further avoidance, which is risky given the reported withdrawal. It also leaves the problem of changing work schedules partially unresolved, as rigid standards might clash with dynamic availability. I propose testing whether conflict decreases when completion criteria are made explicit, even if the assignment method remains informal. This test relies on the assumption that the avoiding friend is willing to engage with a static checklist despite avoiding conversations."
